# CODEOWNERS for a factory PROJECT repo (not the factory itself).
#
# Replace @owner with a real handle. An unresolvable handle protects nothing
# while still looking like protection, which is the most dangerous failure this
# file has — GitHub silently treats an unknown owner as no owner.
#
# NOTE ON PATHS: these must track the real layout. An entry pointing at a path
# that does not exist is the same failure in a different costume. Delete the
# lines that do not apply to this project rather than leaving them aspirational.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is DIFFERENT here from the factory repo
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A project repo has NO verification harness, so there is no `_HARNESS_PATHS`
# mirror and no `substrate-guard`. Do not copy those across. The verifier lives
# in the factory repo and this repo must not be able to reach it.
#
# What stays red here is narrower: the things that decide what "green" means,
# and anything that could quietly change the data a result is computed from.

# CI is the enforcement surface; changing it changes what "green" means.
/.github/workflows/    @owner
/.github/CODEOWNERS    @owner

# Tool config decides what lint/typecheck/test actually enforce.
/pyproject.toml        @owner

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# lane:empirical projects only — delete this block for a deterministic project
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# These are red because they decide what the model is measured ON. An agent that
# can silently widen a split or repin a dataset can move a metric without
# touching a single line of model code, and no gate downstream would see a
# change worth objecting to.

# The split policy. Group-aware splitting is the difference between measuring
# the phenomenon and measuring the recording.
/src/**/splits.py      @owner
/src/**/data/          @owner

# The dataset pin. A SHA change is a different experiment wearing the same name.
/DATASET.lock          @owner
/configs/dataset*.yaml @owner

# The evaluation entrypoint the runner calls. The scorer itself lives in the
# factory repo and holds the labels; this is only what emits predictions, but a
# change here changes what gets scored.
/src/**/predict.py     @owner
